{"schema_version":"blind-4.2.0","winner":"B","margin":"small_but_meaningful","reason":"Both debates build the same core move: the feeling of being behind is separated from the question of what the user wants, and loneliness or drifting from friends is offered as the likely target. B brings a decision-relevant fact into the argument itself: some goods have real time limits, and that changes the Detective's position for a stated cause. A covers deadlines only in its closing map. A is better on trust, because it explicitly flags one mind's extension beyond the source, but that advantage does not outweigh B's edge in reasoning and usefulness for a decision.","comparisons":[{"aspect":"Reasoning and interaction","a_location":"A.debate[9].text","a_observation":"The closing turn mostly restates where each mind now stands ('The Experimenter says act small, the Skeptic says ask people who have done it'). The earlier narrowing in A.debate[3].text and A.debate[5].text is real, but the debate ends on a summary rather than on a new point.","b_location":"B.debate[8].text","b_observation":"The Forecaster objects that indefinite waiting is not neutral because some goods have windows. In B.debate[9].text the Detective concedes for that stated reason and narrows the claim: the feeling tells you little, but the calendar facts can be checked.","why_it_matters":"For someone unsure whether they want marriage or a home, the one thing that could make waiting costly is a real time limit. B gets there through an objection and a concession, so the ending knows something the opening did not."},{"aspect":"Usefulness for this decision","a_location":"A.dependsOn","a_observation":"Gives clear branches: friendship drift, a small trial of settled life, and money or deadline pressure. Deadlines are mentioned only in passing and are never argued.","b_location":"B.dependsOn","b_observation":"Branches on whether the actual life appeals more than the status of having it, on connection versus milestones, and on comparison versus solitude. It names time-limited goods such as children as the decisive fact to learn early, and suggests talking to people who chose none of these paths.","why_it_matters":"B's conditions are more observable and include the path the user is actually considering (not wanting these things), so the user gets a fuller set of options and tradeoffs."},{"aspect":"Trust","a_location":"A.debate[6].text","a_observation":"The Anthropologist marks his bet on belonging as 'my extension, not his claim', keeping Veblen's idea separate from his own reasoning.","b_location":"B.debate[6].text","b_observation":"The Detective's advice to notice private envy is offered as the mind's own suggestion, without saying whether it extends Girard. The Experimenter's reply in B.debate[7].text reads this as withdrawing from friends, which slightly overstates what was said.","why_it_matters":"A is more careful to separate what the source says from what a mind adds, but the gap is small and does not change the overall preference."}],"best_of_a":{"location":"A.debate[5].text","observation":"The Skeptic warns that small trials carry the same forecasting bias (a trial of settled life may feel flat because it is new, or lonely because done alone), then asks the question that sorts the next step: envy, fear of drifting, or money."},"best_of_b":{"location":"B.debate[4].text","observation":"The Forecaster separates the milestone from the good it promises (a home standing in for security, a wedding for being chosen) and suggests those goods may be reachable by other roads, which reframes the choice for someone unsure about the milestones themselves."},"concerns":[{"side":"B","type":"format","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"B.debate[2]","description":"Mill is marked pd=true only on Turn 3, which carries the quotation, and pd=false on his later turns, so the marking is inconsistent across turns for the same source.","evidence_ids":[]},{"side":"B","type":"grounding","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"B.debate[7].text","description":"The Experimenter rejects 'withdrawing from friends', but the Detective in B.debate[6].text suggested noticing private envy, not withdrawing, so the reply answers a slightly stronger claim than the one made.","evidence_ids":[]}]}
